\section{Update to hail proxy}
\label{sec:proxy-update}

The instability-shear proxy of \cite{Raupach_MWR_2023} was slightly updated for
this study, to be more globally applicable. This proxy indicates hail-prone
conditions when

\begin{eqnarray}
    \textrm{MLCAPE}_{100} \times {S_{06}}^\alpha & \geq & \beta, \\
    \textrm{LR} & < & -5.45 \textrm{ K km}^{-1} \label{eq:extra_conditions_1} \\
    \textrm{LI}_{100} & < & 0.4 \textrm{ K} \label{eq:extra_conditions_2} \\
    T_{500} & < & 265.46 \textrm{ K} \label{eq:extra_conditions_3},
\end{eqnarray}

where MLCAPE$_{100}$ (J kg$^{-1}$) is convective available potential energy
calculated using a well-mixed parcel over the lowest 100 hPa of the atmosphere,
$S_{06}$ (m s$^{-1}$) is 0-6 km bulk vertical wind shear, $\alpha$ and $\beta$
are parameters that vary with the melting level height, LR (K km$^{-1}$) is the
lapse rate between 700 hPa and 500 hPa, LI$_{100}$ (K) is the lifted index
defined using same parcel as that used by MLCAPE$_{100}$, and $T_{500}$ (K) is
the temperature at 500 hPa \citep{Raupach_MWR_2023}. 

The idea behind the two parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is that there is a
latitudinal dependence on the proportion of instability versus shear in
environments in which hailstorms occur in Australia \citep{Raupach_MWR_2023}.
Storms in the higher-latitude south, where shear is generally high but melting
level height is low, are more easily discriminated by MLCAPE values, while
storms in the tropics, where MLCAPE and melting level height are both generally
high, are more easily discriminated by shear \citep{Raupach_MWR_2023}. The proxy
uses melting level height (MLH, in m) to adjust the values of $\alpha$ and
$\beta$ and therefore to adjust the discriminator line between hail and no-hail
conditions in instability-shear space.

Figure \ref{fig:proxy_by_MLH} shows the original \cite{Raupach_MWR_2023} proxy
discriminator lines for various values of melting level height (MLH) above the
surface. When the MLH is below about 1500 m, the value of $\alpha$ becomes
negative and the value of $\beta$ becomes very small, and the proxy stops
detecting high-CAPE, high-shear conditions. So that the proxy is more reliable
in colder regions, in this work we have modified the proxy such that when the
MLH is below 2000 m, the values of $\alpha$ and $\beta$ for an MLH of 2000 m are
used. The equations for $\alpha$ and $\beta$ used here are thus

\begin{figure*}[!ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../results/supplementary/proxy_by_MLH}
    \caption{\textbf{Original proxy for various values of melting level height
    (MLH).} CAPE stands for convective available potential energy. $S_{06}$ is
    0-6 km bulk vertical wind shear. The same discriminator lines are shown on
    logarithmic and linear scales.}
    \label{fig:proxy_by_MLH}
\end{figure*}

\begin{equation}
\alpha = 
    \begin{cases} 
        0.00127 \times \textrm{MLH} - 1.88272 & \textrm{ if }\quad\textrm{MLH} \geq 2000 \textrm{ m} \\
        0.00127 \times 2000 - 1.88272 & \textrm{ if }\quad\textrm{MLH} < 2000 \textrm{ m}\\
    \end{cases},
\end{equation}

and 

\begin{equation}
\log_{10}(\beta) =
    \begin{cases}
    0.00204 \times \textrm{MLH} - 1.75631 & \textrm{ if }\quad\textrm{MLH} \geq 2000 \textrm{ m} \\
    0.00204 \times 2000 - 1.75631 & \textrm{ if }\quad\textrm{MLH} < 2000 \textrm{ m} \\
    \end{cases}.
\end{equation}

With and without the slight modification for MLH $< 2000$, the three original
``extra conditions'' defined in \cite{Raupach_MWR_2023} (and here in Equations
\ref{eq:extra_conditions_1}, \ref{eq:extra_conditions_2}, and
\ref{eq:extra_conditions_3}) are similarly effective in removing false positives
from the whole proxy training data set. To assess the impact of this change we
have tested on two datasets:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item On the original training dataset from \cite{Raupach_MWR_2023}: proxy
    performance metrics for the proxy with no extra conditions are similar
    between versions while the performance of the proxy with extra conditions is
    not affected by the change.
    \item On the climatology data used in \cite{Raupach_npjCAS_2023} covering
    storm-prone hours of the day from 1979-2022 across all of Australia: the
    proxy changes affect the ocean areas in the south and southeast of the
    domain. However, over the whole map no location had more than
    \Sexpr{ceiling(as.numeric(results_proxy_update['max_percent_diff_per_pixel_all'])*100)/100}\%
    \% of its values affected and over land no location had more than
    \Sexpr{ceiling(as.numeric(results_proxy_update['max_percent_diff_per_pixel_land'])*100)/100}\%
    of its values affected. The climatology and trends calculated using either
    version of the proxy are very similar.
\end{enumerate}

It is also notable that the projected changes in the Raupach proxy that we
report in this work are not driven primarily by the ``extra conditions'', as
shown by Figure \ref{fig:Raupach_extra_conds} here.

\section{Proxy formulas}
\label{sec:proxy-formulas}

In addition to the Raupach proxy defined in Section \ref{sec:proxy-update}, we
used the proxy of \cite{Eccel_IJC_2012}, wherein an environment is considered
hail prone when 

\begin{eqnarray}
\textrm{MLCAPE}_{100} \times S_{06} > 10000,&\textrm{and} \\
\textrm{MLCIN}_{100} > -50,
\end{eqnarray}

where MLCIN$_{100}$ (J kg$^{-1}$) is the 
convective inhibition, for a well-mixed parcel over the lowest 100
hPa. We also used the
significant hail parameter \citep[SHIP,][]{SPC_SHIP} which is defined by
calculating an initial value SHIP$_i$ as

\begin{equation}
\textrm{SHIP}_i = \frac{\textrm{MUCAPE} \times \textrm{MR} \times \textrm{LR} \times -T_{500} \times S_{06}}{42000000},
\label{eq:SHIP}
\end{equation}

where MUCAPE and MUCIN (both J kg$^{-1}$) are the convective available potential
energy and convective inhibition for the ``most unstable'' parcel, defined as
the parcel with the largest equivalent potential temperature over the lowest 250
hPa of the atmosphere, MR (g kg$^{-1}$) is the mixing ratio for that parcel, and
$T_{500}$ is the temperature at 500 hPa in degrees C. SHIP$_i$ is only defined
when 

\begin{eqnarray}
7 \leq \textrm{S06} \leq 27 \textrm{ m s}^{-1}, & \textrm{and} \\
11 \leq \textrm{MR} \leq 13.6 \textrm{ g kg}^{-1}, &
\end{eqnarray}

and if $T_{500} > -5.5$ degrees C, then a value of $T_{500} = -5.5$ is used.
Once SHIP$_i$ has been calculated using Equation \ref{eq:SHIP} and these
constraints, the following modifications are made to get the final value of
SHIP:

\begin{eqnarray}
\textrm{SHIP} = \textrm{SHIP}_i \times \frac{\textrm{MUCAPE}}{1300}, & \textrm{if MUCAPE} < 1300 \textrm{ J kg}^{-1} \\ 
\textrm{SHIP} = \textrm{SHIP}_i \times \frac{\textrm{LR}}{5.8}, & \textrm{if LR} < 5.8 \textrm{ K km}^{-1} \\
\textrm{SHIP} = \textrm{SHIP}_i \times \frac{\textrm{FLH}}{2400}, & \textrm{if FLH} < 2400 \textrm{ m AGL},
\end{eqnarray}

where FLH is freezing-level height (height of dry-bulb zero degrees C) and AGL
stands for above ground level (\cite{Raupach_MWR_2023} used FLH above sea level
for Australia, but here we use FLH AGL as per both the original SHIP definition
and \cite{Prein_WCE_2018}). We considered an environment to be hail prone when
SHIP $> 0.1$, as per \cite{Raupach_MWR_2023}.

\section{Validation of proxy climatologies}

For global climatologies we compare to those of \cite{Prein_WCE_2018}, which is
proxy-based, and the satellite-based climatology of \cite{Bang_JAMC_2019}, while
noting that both these comparison global climatologies are for severe or large
hail, and the proxies we use are designed to detect any hail-prone conditions.
For each region, we present the minimum, mean and maximum number of annual
hail-prone days over the model-proxy ensemble for the historical period,
including both the CMIP6 models and ERA5 reanalysis for three proxies (27
ensemble members), and compare to the global and local climatologies. In most
regions, at least some of the proxies used here show high hail probability over
the ocean which is not reproduced in the \cite{Prein_WCE_2018} and
\cite{Bang_JAMC_2019} climatologies. We consider the greater inter-proxy spread
over the oceans to reflect the higher uncertainty with respect to hail-prone
conditions over maritime areas than land areas \citep{Sari_JGRA_2025,
Knight_2001} and focus mainly on land comparisons in the below analyses.

\todo{Generally agreement is strong, with the exception of the Tibetan Plateau
and the tropical oceans, and the highest hail risk region in the USA}

We do not attempt to compare the number of hail days per year because of its
dependance on the support over which it is calculated.

\subsection{Asia}

\begin{figure}[!h]
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../results/supplementary/proxy_climatology_asia}
\caption{Minimum, mean, and maximum annual hail-prone days across the model-proxy ensemble for the historical period, including both CMIP6 models and ERA5 reanalysis, for Asia.}
\label{fig:proxy_climatology_asia}
\end{figure}

In Asia, our ensemble (Figure \ref{fig:proxy_climatology_asia}) highlights the
southern flank of the Himalaya and particularly its southeastern end between as
the most hail prone region, with another hotspot in the Guangdong and Guangxi
provinces of China, and low hail probability across the Tibetan plateau. This
pattern matches that shown in \cite{Bang_JAMC_2019} satellite data, and is
similar to that in \cite{Prein_WCE_2018} except that \cite{Prein_WCE_2018} shows
another hotspot in the northeast of Pakistan which is not shown in either the
proxies used here or the \cite{Bang_JAMC_2019} climatology. Regional studies
confirm that the north-western Himalaya \citep{Bhat_NH_2024}, North, Northeast,
and East India \citep{Rao_NH_2025}, Northern Thailand \cite{Mahavik_NH_2025},
and Southwest China \citep{Li_JAMC_2018} are hail hotspots. Hail is also
reported across some parts of Central India \citep{Rao_NH_2025,
Ravindren_NH_2025} where the global satellite and proxy products show only
moderate hail probability. Station-based climatologies in China show high hail
occurrence over the Tibetan Plateau \citep{Zhang_JAMC_2008, Li_JAMC_2018},
disagreeing with global proxy and satellite studies.

\FloatBarrier

\subsection{Oceania}

\begin{figure}[!h]
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../results/supplementary/proxy_climatology_oceania}
\caption{As for Figure \ref{fig:proxy_climatology_asia} but for Oceania.}
\label{fig:proxy_climatology_oceania}
\end{figure}

In Oceania, our proxy results (Figure \ref{fig:proxy_climatology_oceania}) agree
with the locations of known hail hotspots. \citep{Prein_WCE_2018} and
\cite{Bang_JAMC_2019} both show regions of higher hail probability on the mid
eastern and northern coasts of Australia. Our proxies show hail probability also
in the southwest of Australia, which agrees with the \citep{Prein_WCE_2018}
climatology. \cite{Bang_JAMC_2019} shows low but non-zero hail probability
across the western Maritime Continent, agreeing with our global proxies but
disagreeing with \cite{Prein_WCE_2018}. A maxima in hail probability off the
east coast of Australia is shown in our proxies and \cite{Prein_WCE_2018}, but
not replicated by satellite detections \citep{Bang_JAMC_2019}. Our proxies and
that of \cite{Prein_WCE_2018} agree on the North Island of New Zealand showing
higher hail probability than the South Island. Regional studies confirm the hail
peak on Australia's east coast \citep{Raupach_npjCAS_2023, Brook_MWR_2024} and
the possibility of a hail hotspot in the southwest \citep{Raupach_npjCAS_2023,
Brook_MWR_2024, Bedka_JAMC_2018}, while hail in Australia's tropics is subject
to uncertainty \citep{Raupach_MWR_2023, Bedka_JAMC_2018, Prein_WCE_2018}. In New
Zealand, hailstorm occurrence is recorded as increasing towards the west and
south \citep{Steiner_NZJGG_1989}, but recent data are scarce. Damaging
hailstorms occur in Indonesia \citep{Sari_JGRA_2025}.

\FloatBarrier

\subsection{North America}

\begin{figure}[!h]
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../results/supplementary/proxy_climatology_north_america}
\caption{As for Figure \ref{fig:proxy_climatology_asia} but for North America.}
\label{fig:proxy_climatology_north_america}
\end{figure}

Our proxy ensemble (Figure \ref{fig:proxy_climatology_north_america}) shows
hail-prone areas in North America to the east of the Rocky Mountains with the
highest occurrence of hail-prone conditions in the southeast United States,
along the Gulf of Mexico and eastern Florida; hail-prone conditions extend into
north into Canada to about 50 \degr{} and further in Alberta, and south into
Central America, with a thin region of increased probability along the west
coast of Mexico. The severe hail climatology of \cite{Prein_WCE_2018} highlights
a similar region of risk and agrees with increased hail risk in Mexico's west,
albeit a little further inland. However, \cite{Prein_WCE_2018} has the highest
probability area in the USA further west and across the Great Plains.
Satellite-based detections also highlight similar regions as our proxies, but
put the greatest occurrence across the US Great Plains and Cuba. \todo{Do
individual proxies pick out the Gulf area?}. Regional climatologies confirm that
the most hail-prone areas of the United States is the Great Plains east of the
Rocky Mountains, and that there is a strong east-west divide with little hail
occurrence to the west of the Rocky Mountains \citep{Cintineo_WF_2012,
Allen_EJSSM_2015, Allen_JAMES_2015, Murillo_MWR_2021}. A radar-based climatology
shows outlier hotspots in Florida and the eastern sides of Georgia and South
Carolina \citep{Cintineo_WF_2012}, and hail reports exist in most areas east of
the Rocky Mountains \citep{Allen_JAMES_2015}. In Canada, the highest hail
probability region is across southern Manitoba and Saskatchewan and extending
northwards into Alberta \citep{Brunet_JAMC_2024} which agrees well with our
ensemble climatology. In Mexico, hailstorms occur over the high-elevation parts
of the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt in the south of the country and the Sierra
Madre Occidental in the north west \citep{Leon-Cruz_IJC_2023}. The occurrence of
hail over the Sierra Madre Occidental aligns with our ensemble's increased hail
probability in the northwest coastal region of Mexico. 

